Exercise: Nominal Data and Distance Measures






Q5. You cluster 10 English dialects on 20 binary phonological features using Euclidean distance on the 0/1 matrix. Your colleague says Jaccard distance would have been more appropriate. What is the key difference, and does it matter here?





Euclidean distance treats shared absences (0,0) the same as shared presences (1,1) when computing similarity. Jaccard ignores shared absences, giving more weight to shared presences. For sparse binary linguistic data (many features absent in most dialects), Jaccard is more appropriate because shared absences are less linguistically informative than shared presences.




Jaccard distance is only appropriate for text data — for dialect features, Euclidean is always preferred




Euclidean distance cannot be computed on binary data — it is only valid for continuous variables




There is no difference — Euclidean and Jaccard distance produce identical results for binary data

